Print the tabs for a tab in a run of blanks as soon as it is read.
unexpand added up the columns of a run of blanks and printed its tabs
only when it reached the text after the run. When the run ended in a
tab at the end of the string, the loop ended with the tabs still
pending, so they were dropped. A line ending in a newline was fine,
since the newline counts as text, but an unterminated last line such
as "x\t" or "  \t", or a line cut short by a NUL, lost its trailing
blanks. The sum could also wrap with a large tab size, so tabs were
lost even in a line ending in a newline: "\t\tx" lost both tabs with
-t 2147483648, and "\t\t\tx" all three with -t 2147483647.

A tab always reaches the next tab stop, so print the tabs owed so far
when the tab is read, and count again from that stop. Nothing is then
pending at the end of the string, and the count stays below the tab
size plus the spaces since the last tab.

---
 coreutils/expand.c |    7 ++++++-
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/coreutils/expand.c
+++ b/coreutils/expand.c
@@ -148,7 +148,12 @@
 			}
 			column += len;
 			if (*ptr == '\t') {
-				column += tab_size - (column % tab_size);
+				/* Reaches the next tab stop: print the tabs
+				 * now, since adding up columns could overflow */
+				n = column / tab_size + 1;
+				column = 0;
+				while (n--)
+					putchar('\t');
 				ptr++;
 				continue;
 			}
